I can't make the agents think faster, but I've cut the waiting and the process overhead. All P0 deliverables stay in scope.

- **Running steps:** I told the design review to wrap up within a few minutes. I also gave the AWS spike a 10-minute time box and told it not to create a CloudFront distribution or an AgentCore runtime, since those are slow to create and clean up. Both will still write their outputs.
- **Planner directive:** the design review will put a speed directive at the top of its output, so the planner reads it first and copies it into every step it writes. It says:
  - run more narrowly scoped parallel workstreams;
  - deploy the base stack early in the background;
  - tune the agent locally and deploy the AgentCore runtime once;
  - use one review pass per area that blocks only on serious issues, with the loop capped at 2 and no second-model reviewers;
  - run each eval scenario once first, and three times only if the first pass is green;
  - do one browser check pass and a time-boxed final verification.
- **`aldo.txt`:** I told both running agents not to open it, and the directive tells the planner to carry that rule into every step it writes.

I kept the security checks and the real-DynamoDB check for concurrent execution, because those are the core of the demo. The cost of the trimmed review is that some bugs may slip through. They'll be listed as known issues in the final report.

I can't compress a few fixed waits: CloudFront creation (5–15 minutes), the AgentCore runtime build, and the live Bedrock eval runs. I also can't give you an ETA yet. The planner hasn't started, and its decomposition will show how parallel the build really is.

If you want it faster still, the biggest saving would be dropping the live eval and the Impact tab. Both are P0 requirements (R13 and R14), so I haven't cut them without your say.

